<template>
  <screen class="app">
    <text class="title">a modal</text>
    <text class="lead">A dialog over a scrim, both animated, dismissed by tapping the scrim or by Cancel. The page behind it stays exactly where it was.</text>

    <view class="btn" @tap="open = true">
      <text class="btn-label">delete the archive</text>
    </view>

    <text class="result">{{ result }}</text>

    <!-- Filler, so there is something to see the scrim laid over, and so the
         page behind is visibly not moved by the dialog arriving. -->
    <view class="filler" r-for="n in [1, 2, 3, 4]">
      <text class="filler-text">a row of the page underneath</text>
    </view>

    <!-- The scrim is the whole modal. Positioned over everything, it is what
         takes the tap that dismisses, and `r-transition` on it animates the
         scrim and the dialog together as one arrival.

         There is no `position: fixed` in Rux, so the cover is `absolute` and
         the box it is measured against is the nearest positioned ancestor.
         That is what `position: relative` on `.app` below is for, and giving
         the scrim all four insets is what makes it the same size. -->
    <view class="scrim" r-if="open" r-transition @tap="dismiss()">
      <!-- **The dialog needs its own `@tap` and it does nothing on purpose.**
           A tap goes to the topmost element that *has a handler* and stops
           there: there is no propagation, so there is nothing to stop. A box
           with no handler is not a tap target at all, so without this line a
           tap on the dialog falls straight through to the scrim behind it and
           closes the thing you were trying to use.

           It is not free. A `@tap` box is an interactive element, so the dialog
           takes the focus ring when it is tapped and becomes a Tab stop that
           does nothing. There is no way to say "tappable but not focusable"
           today. Worth knowing before deciding a scrim should dismiss at all:
           a Cancel button costs nothing and needs no swallow. -->
      <view class="dialog" @tap="0">
        <text class="dialog-title">Delete the archive?</text>
        <text class="dialog-body">Everything in it goes with it. This cannot be undone, which is the only reason it is worth asking.</text>
        <view class="dialog-row">
          <view class="btn quiet" @tap="dismiss()">
            <text class="btn-label">cancel</text>
          </view>
          <view class="btn danger" @tap="confirm()">
            <text class="btn-label">delete</text>
          </view>
        </view>
      </view>
    </view>
  </screen>
</template>

<style>
  .app {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 20px;
    background: #1e1e2e;
    /* What the scrim's insets are measured against. Without it the scrim would
       climb to the screen, which happens to be the same box here and would stop
       being so the moment this modal was written inside anything else. */
    position: relative;
  }

  .title { color: #cdd6f4; font-size: 22px; font-weight: 700; }
  .lead { color: #a6adc8; font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px; }
  .result { color: #a6e3a1; font-size: 13px; height: 18px; }

  .filler { width: 100%; padding: 12px; background: #313244; border-radius: 8px; }
  .filler-text { color: #6c7086; font-size: 13px; }

  .btn {
    padding: 10px 16px;
    background: #89b4fa;
    border-radius: 10px;
    cursor: pointer;
    align-self: flex-start;
  }

  .btn:hover { background: #b4befe; }
  .btn-label { color: #1e1e2e; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 700; }
  .quiet { background: #45475a; }
  .quiet:hover { background: #585b70; }
  .quiet .btn-label { color: #cdd6f4; }
  .danger { background: #f38ba8; }
  .danger:hover { background: #eba0ac; }

  /* Fixed to the window and given every inset, so it covers whatever is under
     it regardless of where in the template it was written. The flex centring
     is what puts the dialog in the middle. */
  .scrim {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    background: #11111bcc;
    opacity: 1;
    transition: opacity 180ms ease-out;
  }

  /* The scrim fades. Note what is *not* here: no `position: absolute` on
     `:leave-to`. A departing box keeps the place it would have had, and this
     one is already out of the flow, so it has no place to give up and nothing
     below it to disturb. That rule is for a box that was in the flow. */
  .scrim:enter-from { opacity: 0; }
  .scrim:leave-to { opacity: 0; }

  .dialog {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
    width: 340px;
    padding: 20px;
    background: #313244;
    border: 2px solid #45475a;
    border-radius: 14px;
    transform: translateY(0px) scale(1);
    transition: transform 180ms ease-out;
  }

  /* The dialog moves while the scrim fades. Two elements, two rules, one swap:
     the swap is opened by the `r-if` on the scrim, and everything inside it is
     carried along by the same `:enter-from` / `:leave-to` pass. */
  .scrim:enter-from .dialog { transform: translateY(12px) scale(0.96); }
  .scrim:leave-to .dialog { transform: translateY(12px) scale(0.96); }

  .dialog-title { color: #cdd6f4; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 700; }
  .dialog-body { color: #a6adc8; font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px; }
  .dialog-row { display: flex; flex-direction: row; gap: 8px; justify-content: flex-end; }
</style>

<script>
  let open = signal(false);
  let result = signal("");

  fn dismiss() {
    open = false;
    result = "left alone";
  }

  fn confirm() {
    open = false;
    result = "archive deleted";
  }
</script>
